# A breakpoint condition the debugger can run in the program does: setting
# it says so, and a hit where it is false runs on without a stop, so a
# million such hits take the program under a second (it times them itself).
# The condition `i == 3 && n == 5` is false through count_up(1000000) and
# holds once in count_up(5), which stops with `i` at 3. A condition that
# reads more locals than a call's registers hold runs in the program too,
# and stops in sums with `i` at 2. A condition that builds a List can't run
# in the program: setting it says so, the debugger checks it at each hit,
# and it still stops only where it holds, with `i` at 3.
# The fixture must stay in jet fmt form: line 6 is the first statement of
# count_up's loop body, and lines 20 and 21 are sums'.
fixture: ConditionalBreakpointRunsInProcess.fixture
env: NO_COLOR=1
env: PATH={work}/no-tools
step: jet debug prog.jet
stdin: conditional_breakpoint_runs_in_process.stdin
exit: 0
stdout: ~ "Breakpoint set  prog.jet:6 if i == 3 && n == 5\nThe condition runs in the program: a hit where it is false runs on without a stop\n" # the condition runs in the program
stdout: ~ "Breakpoint set  prog.jet:20 if a + b + c + d + e + i == 17\nThe condition runs in the program: a hit where it is false runs on without a stop\n" # a condition of six locals runs in the program too
stdout: ~ "Breakpoint set  prog.jet:21 if [i, 2][0] == 3\nThe condition can't run in the program: " # a condition that can't says so when it is set
stdout: ~ "; the debugger checks it at each hit, which stops the program each time\n" # and says the debugger checks it instead
stdout: ~ "a million hits under a second\n" # a million false hits run on at full speed
stdout: !~ "i = 0\n" # no condition stops where it is false
stdout: ~ "Breakpoint hit  prog.jet:6  in count_up()\n" # the placed condition stops where it holds
stdout: ~ "print i\ni = 3\n" # with the local it held for
stdout: ~ "Breakpoint hit  prog.jet:20  in sums()\n" # the six-local condition stops where it holds
stdout: ~ "print i\ni = 2\n" # with its local
stdout: ~ "Breakpoint hit  prog.jet:21  in sums()\n" # the checked condition stops where it holds
stdout: ~ "print i\ni = 3\n" # with its local
stdout: ~ " 10 66\n" # both functions run to their totals
stdout: ~ "Program finished\n" # the program runs to its end
